{"causes":["Written during the Russian Revolution, the book was influenced by the ideas of Vladimir Lenin and Leon Trotsky.","Published in the aftermath of World War I, the book tapped into widespread disillusionment with capitalist systems.","Emerging from the intellectual currents of early 20th-century socialism, the book's authors drew on a range of sources, including Marxist theory and revolutionary experience."],"effects":["Influenced the development of Marxist-Leninist ideology and socialist thought.","Contributed to the rise of communist parties and states in the 20th century.","Remains a relevant work for scholars and activists today, providing insight into the theoretical underpinnings of communism."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"vladimir-lenin","sourceName":"Vladimir Lenin","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"Lenin's ideas on state socialism and the role of the vanguard party in the revolution."},{"sourceSlug":"leon-trotsky","sourceName":"Leon Trotsky","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"Trotsky's theories on permanent revolution and the role of the working class in socialist transformation."},{"sourceSlug":"world-war-i","sourceName":"World War I","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The global crisis of the 1910s and the resulting crisis of confidence in capitalist systems."},{"sourceSlug":"russian-revolution","sourceName":"Russian Revolution","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The revolution's impact on Russian society and politics, and the emergence of a new socialist order."},{"sourceSlug":"marxist-theory","sourceName":"Marxist Theory","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The book's engagement with the key concepts and ideas of Marxist thought, including dialectical materialism and the critique of capitalism."}],"places":[{"name":"Moscow","role":"Major battle"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Black Book of Communism","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-black-book-of-communism"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q334896","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Black_Book_of_Communism"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Black Book of Communism contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Black Book of Communism: Crimes, Terror, Repression (French: Le Livre noir du communisme: Crimes, terreur, répression) is a controversial scholarly anthology edited by the French historian Stéphane Courtois and co-authored by six other historians (Nicolas Werth, Jean-Louis Panné, Andrzej Paczkowski, Karel Bartošek, Jean-Louis Margolin, and Rémi Kauffer), published by Harvard University Press in French in 1997 and translated into many languages (English 1999). The book is an attempt to document, in systematic comparative form, the crimes and deaths attributable to communist regimes worldwide — the Soviet Union, China, Cambodia, Korea, Vietnam, Eastern Europe, Cuba, Ethiopia, and elsewhere — and arrives at a controversial aggregate estimate of approximately 94–100 million deaths attributable to communist regimes in the 20th century.

The Black Book of Communism generated an immediate and sustained controversy, both about its methods and about its political implications. The aggregate death toll estimate (94–100 million) has been contested by other historians (Robert Conquest, whose research on Soviet terror Courtois drew on, and others have given lower estimates for some regimes); the book's co-authors publicly disagreed with Courtois's framing and his political agenda (several co-authors wrote prefaces distancing themselves from Courtois's comparison of communism to Nazism in body count terms); and critics argued that the aggregation of death tolls across diverse regimes over eight decades distorts more than it illuminates.

Despite the controversy, The Black Book of Communism became one of the most widely read works on communist history and an important document in the post-Cold War reassessment of the communist legacy — particularly in Eastern Europe and France, where it provoked intense public debate about the relationship between the French Communist Party and Stalinist repression.","subjects":["History","Modern Era","Communism","Soviet History","Political History","Historiography","Cold War","20th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Black Book of Communism played a significant role in the development of Marxist-Leninist ideology and socialist thought, influencing the rise of communist parties and states in the 20th century and remaining a relevant work for scholars and activists today.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
